WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.220
We hope you're near the Mos Eisley Cantina,
because you'll definitely need to unwind after

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.740
hearing this head-spinning new Star Wars theory.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.880
"Welcome, young Skywalker!"

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.820
Over at Screen Rant, writer Thomas Bacon has
published a theory centered around Emperor

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.400
Palpatine, the Sith Lord who rose through
the ranks of the Galactic Republic to become

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.480
its Emperor before Darth Vader took him out
in Return of the Jedi by launching him into

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.740
the Death Star's reactor.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.540
Or, at least, that's what we thought happened.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.770
The vicious villain is slated to make a jaw-dropping
comeback in writer-director J.J. Abrams' The

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:34.940
Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.740
Palpatine's signature cackle was heard in
the first trailer, and his presence could

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.210
be felt in the final pre-release footage for
the film, when Daisy Ridley's Rey stood opposite

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.220
a dark figure.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.320
Many have speculated as to what Emperor Palpatine's
role in The Rise of Skywalker will be, without

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.540
accounting for how he would even be alive
to return and wreak havoc in the first place.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.600
Now, Bacon has presented a theory apparently
crafted by many other Star Wars fans as to

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.180
how the character survived his apparent death-by-the-Death-Star.

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:06.500
The idea goes that Emperor Palpatine didn't
really die when Darth Vader, overcome with

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.740
emotion while watching Palpatine attack and
torture his son Luke Skywalker with Force

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.420
lightning, chucked him into the Death Star's
reactor.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.220
Rather, it's believed that Palpatine transformed
himself into Force energy to survive the fall

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.180
into the reactor.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.780
And that energy blast may not have been a
release of Dark Side energy as traditionally

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:28.160
believed, but instead Palpatine himself in
his new Force energy form.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.200
How exactly might have Emperor Palpatine rid
himself of his mortal body and turned himself

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.940
into intangible Force energy?

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.660
The theory points to the in-universe legends
collection Star Wars: Myths &amp; Fables, which

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.140
contains a story called "Gaze of Stone."

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.900
The story involves a Sith Lord apprentice
performing a ritual that transforms flesh.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.790
His plan went sideways, however, when the
ritual was turned against him, and he was

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:51.840
turned into rock.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.840
According to this new theory, Emperor Palpatine
used a Sith ritual similar to this one to

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.320
escape death at the end of Return of the Jedi.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.740
Palpatine's preoccupation with immortality
is an important foundation of this theory,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.890
as the ensuing details claim that Palpatine
built his Throne Rooms like the one within

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.190
the Death Star in a way that would allow him
to "amplify his power" and "transform into

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.260
an energy state rather than die."

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:18.720
It isn't out of the realm of possibility that
Emperor Palpatine, fearful of dying and losing

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.640
power over the galaxy, would call upon a Sith
enchantment to cheat death.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.780
However, there's a big snag in this theory:
it doesn't explain how Palpatine would then

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.489
return to his physical form pale skin, haunting
eyes, and all to stir up trouble in The Rise

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:33.360
of Skywalker.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.000
Many have turned to Chuck Wendig's Star Wars:
Aftermath novels for a possible solution.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.470
The books feature a Sith cultist and former
adviser to Emperor Palpatine who attempts

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.690
a ceremony within one of the Emperor's Throne
Rooms in an attempt to revive Palpatine and

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:47.520
restore his body.

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:52.300
The ceremony fails in the novel, but could
someone else carry out a similar ceremony

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.320
in The Rise of Skywalker and place Palpatine
back in his corporeal form?

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.640
Anything's possible in the Star Wars universe,
but the ritual-performer would have to be

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:04.840
someone extremely devoted to the dark side
and the advancement of the First Order who

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.700
would genuinely want to see the Emperor return
to the throne.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:13.900
An obvious choice would be Kylo Ren, the emotionally-unbalanced
villain who may be suffering a crisis of confidence

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.620
and faith in the upcoming movie.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.070
Plus, there have been a ton of rumors suggesting
that Kylo Ren will abandon the dark side and

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:24.560
his villainous moniker before the end of The
Rise of Skywalker, reclaiming his birth name

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.030
of Ben Solo to complete his redemption arc.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.460
A potentially faltering First Order baddie
isn't exactly the kind of candidate one would

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.640
select to resurrect Emperor Palpatine and
bring about what's probably going to be the

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.260
most intense First-Order-versus-Resistance
battle the galaxy has ever seen.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.720
Unless, of course, Kylo revives him for the
sole purpose of killing him for good this

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:45.220
time.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.680
Fans will have to try to keep their minds
from running wild with unconfirmed theories,

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:54.430
and patiently wait until The Rise of Skywalker
opens in theaters on December 20 to learn

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:54.980
the truth.

